  "summary_en": "Opinion of the German Ethics Council on AI and the human-machine relationship, framed around whether delegating tasks to machines extends or diminishes human authorship and responsibility. It analyses medicine, schools, public communication and administration, and issues sector-specific and ten cross-cutting recommendations. Robots appear only marginally (surgical robots, care).",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Machines cannot act or bear responsibility; responsibility stays with humans and institutions, and robot personhood is rejected.",
   "AI should support decisions, not replace them, to prevent diffusion of responsibility and automation bias.",
   "Complete replacement of doctors or teachers by AI is ethically unjustifiable.",
   "Calls for certification, audits and oversight of medical AI, plus binding transparency and anti-discrimination requirements.",
   "Ten cross-cutting recommendations cover data sovereignty, critical infrastructure resilience, dual-use and bias."
  ],
